>ART.DSC
art.lsp creates arced text through a dialogue box. Unlike
many similar routines the user doesn't need to select an
arc or circle to create the arced text. Just select a 
center point, radius, check a radio button which specifies
whether the text is to be drawn facing the center or
facing away from the center and the type in the text at
the edit line. Donation $15
<END ART.DSC


>MATT.DSC
matt.lsp moves attributes inside blocks using the
insertion point of the attribute as the base point.
This routine lets you select and move one attribute
at a time until you press the spacebar.(equivalent
to the 'attedit' command in autocadd but much easier
to use and more user friendly. Donation $5
<END MATT.DESC

>ENEDIT.DSC
enedit.lsp is a powerful entity editor for autocadd. 
This program allows the user to select one sample 
entity and the from that entity select all attributes
(i.e. color,layer,style,etc.) to create a selection
set of entities from. From that selection set the user
can then run a number of autocadd commands or enedit
routines to modify it. Example: Say you want all text
on layer ----TX to be 0.12 units high. First you'd 
select the 'Select Sample Entity<' button and select
a text entity on layer '----TX'. Then you'd select the
'Chstyle' option on the 'Edit:' popup list, then on the
'SSby' scroll lists select 'Layer' and 'Type' (at least
one of these lists must be selected). Then select the
'Edit' button. At the Autocadd command line you will see
a list of options to select for the chstyle command, you 
want to change height so type 'h' <enter>. Now at the 
autocadd command line you'll see 'select objects', you 
can either select entities one by one, window an area,
or just type 'all' <enter>. Only those entities that 
follow the selection set criteria that you specified
will be selected and edited - in this case all text
on layer '----TX' will now have a height of 0.12!(Other
edits its capable of -change slant, style, font, height,
move, scale, erase, copy, block, width, all commands in
the autocad change command.) Donation $20
<END ENEDIT.DSC

>FBLK.DSC
fblk.lsp is a program that gets user input on which block
to find and the zoom window size. The routine will then 
find each user specified block and zoom to it using the 
user specified zoom window. It pauses at each block found 
until the user hits the spacebar or <enter> button. 
Donation $5
<END FBLK.DSC

>SCBL.DSC
scbl.lsp gets user input on what the block scale should 
be, then the user selects which block to scale and the 
routine will scale that block about its insertion point to
the user specified scale, the routine continues scaling 
blocks one at a time until the user selects an entity that 
isn't a block or presses the spacebar.
Donation $5
<END SCBL.DSC

>HFACE.DSC
hface.lsp is a program that creates a 3dface around text in
a drawing by either user selection, layer (specified by 
user), or all text in the drawing. The 3dface is placed on
layer '----HI' and is placed at a user specified elevation
and scale (scale determines the gap between the text and 
3dface box). Included programs are rid.lsp, pplot.lsp, and
a routine that checks your drawing for null text. Null text
entities is text inside your drawing that has nothing in 
it. They are not visible but when zoom extents is envoked 
the zoom window may be much larger than the visible 
drawing size because autocad is zooming to the null text 
entities. If hface finds a null text entity it places 
'NullText' inside that entity (thus making it visible) 
and warns the user giving him/her the option of manually 
erasing those entities or letting hface erase them. 
Pplot.lsp should be used in paper space only. This routine 
turns hide on for all viewport in your drawing, regardless
if they're on/off or frozen/thawed, it also turns layer
'----HI' off. The rid command erases all 3dfaces on layer
'----HI' so the user can start over and create new 3dfaces
from scratch if the text size has changed or a large  
number of text entities have been moved or erased without 
editing the 3dfaces surrounding them.
Donation $25
<END HFACE.DSC

>CHM.DSC
chm.lsp converts dimension text (in inches) to metric (in
mm). This routine was written to convert all utility pipe 
diameters in inches to mm. This routine works by looking
at every text entity in the current drawing and if a text 
entity follows the convention of pipe size first and then
followed by any other text (optional) the routine zooms
to the offending text at a user specified zoom window and
is asked whether to convert the text or not. If the user's
answer is yes then the text is split at the inch sign (")
and everthing before it is converted to mm and the inch
symbol (") is converted to 'mm'. Example: if the text
18" S.D. was in the drawing the routine would zoom to this
text, at the command line would repeat this text to the
user and ask whether to convert it or not, If the user
answers yes the text would be split so S.D. remains in
the same location but now 18" reads 458 mm (round to 
nearest whole mm). The user is then asked for a new 
location to put the converted text so it can be lined back
up with the S.D. text. This routine continues through the
same sequence until all text containing " has been viewed
by the user. $10
<END CHM.DSC

>CHMET.DSC
chmet.lsp was written to convert all contour text from feet
to metric. This routine asks the user to select sample text
and window an area to convert (or type all) the routine
then converts all text on the specified text layer to 
meters. $10
<END CHMET.DSC 

>DRIVE.DSC
drive.lsp was written for survey data files to draw a 
driveway at the correct location and dimensions automatic-
ally. This routine works by prompting the user to select
the survey file (*.pts) to check. If the survey data file
follows the convention of xx' dw in the description area
for every center point of each driveway surveyed two lines
are drawn at each side of the driveway at xx/2 feet from
the center point of the driveway. I.E. :
1     1000.000     1000.000  120.125   20' DW
2     1500.152     1056.235  115.253   FL  
...
Donation $5
<END DRIVE.DSC

>FL.DSC
fl.lsp was written for survey data files to draw a 
spot elevation at the flowline of all flowlines surveyed
automatically. This routine works by prompting the user to
select the survey file (*.pts) to check. If the survey data
file follows the convention of 'fl' in the description area
and the z section (elevation) is the flowline elevation
a spot elevation will be placed at the correct x,y location
in the current drawing with the correct elevation 
automatically. survey descriptors in the *.pts file must be
*FL* to work properly. I.E.:
1 1000.000 1000.000 121.025 SSFL
2 1520.005 1056.158 128.025 MH
.....
And a block named 'FL' containing one attribute must be difined
i.e.:
        *---FLxxxx    where xxxx is the attribute.
Donation $5
<END FL.DSC

>NUT.DSC
nut.lsp was written for survey data files to draw a 
spot elevation on watermeters in the current drawing
automatically. This routine works by prompting the user to
select the survey file (*.pts) to check. If the survey data
file follows the convention of 'wm' in the description area
and the z section (elevation) is the nut elevation
a spot elevation will be placed at the correct x,y location
in the current drawing with the correct elevation 
automatically. Survey descriptors must in the *.pts file
must read *VA* to work properly.
I.E.:
1 1000.000 1000.000 121.025 WMVA
2 1520.005 1056.158 128.025 MH
.....
And a block named 'NUT' containing one attribute must be difined
i.e.:
        *---NUTxxxx    where xxxx is the attribute.
NOTE:IGNORES ANY DESC. WITH A G IN IT I.E. GASVA
Donation $5
<END NUT.DSC

>ASVIEW.DSC
asview.lsp is an ascii text viewer. It prompts the user
for a text file and then displays it in a dialogue box.
The user is then offered the option to either print it,
close the file, or open another. (as with many of these
routines a similar viewer is offered in softdesk but this
saves the user time and hassle from switching to a new
menu which has a similar routine and then waiting for
that particular module to be loaded into memory-with
this routine just type 'AV' to view.)
Donation $10
<END ASVIEW.DSC

>DI.DSC
di.lsp operates out of a dialogue box and allows the user
to measure lengths of objects drawn in 3d (having x,y,z
coordinates). The user can measure in any dimension and in any
combination of dimensions (x, y, z, xz, xy, etc.).
Donation $10
<END DI.DSC

>CHSL.DSC
chsl.lsp was written for text in profiles written as 
'S=XX.X% to be converted to S=0.00X. It finds all text
following this convention and converts it.
Donation $5
<END CHSL.DSC

>CHEL.DSC
chel.lsp moves 3d entities to 2d (sets z values to zero) by
selection.
Donation $5
<END CHEL.DSC

>STA.DSC
sta.lsp was written for suvey data files. It reads the
stationing supplied by the surveyors in the descriptor area
of the *.pts files and inserts the station and a tick mark
at the surveyed station point. The survey data file must be
space dileminated and follow this example:
1 1000.000 1000.000 125.256 120+23-insert sta 120.23
(note any other data can be in the descriptor as long as
there is a '+' or '&' to signify a station) i.e.:
CL 100+50 in the descriptor would still produce a station.
Donation $5
<END STA.DSC

>REBLK.DSC
reblk.lsp replaces a selected block with a new block by 
selection. First enter in the new block name then select
blocks one at a time that should be converted, press 
spacebar to exit.
Donation $5
<END REBLK.DSC

>ROBL.DSC
robl.lsp rotates blocks by selection using the insertion
point as the base point of rotation.-Type 'RB'
Donation $5
<END ROBL.DSC

>CARE.DSC
care.lsp was written to calculate the areas of elevation
contours for earthwork calcs. It calculates the countours 
by selection and then creates a table showing contour 
elevations and areas.
Donation $5
<END CARE.DSC

>HAR.DSC
har.lsp creates an arced leader with a hatched arrowhead for use
with the hide command when using hface.lsp to hide entities under
text.
DONATION $5
<END HAR.DSC

>SAR.DSC
sar.lsp creates an arced leader with a solid arrowhead.
DONATION $5
<END SAR.DSC

>LDARCH.DSC
ldarch.lsp creates a fancy curved polyline leader using a 
compound curve as the leader line.-Type LA to run.
Donation $5
<END LDARCH.DSC

>MRRD_TXT.DSC
mrrd_txt.lsp unmirrors (if that's a word) text that has been
mirrored for one reason or another.
Donation $5
<END MRRD_TXT.DSC

>XCOPY.DSC
xcopy.lsp copies simple entites (not complex) from a xref'd dwg.
to the user's current drawing.
Donation $5
<END XCOPY.DSC

>IDENT.DSC
ident.lsp is used to determine the type of entity selected in a
xref'd drawing. Just select an entity and the program returns
whether the entity is a circle, line, etc..
Donation $5
<END IDENT.DSC

>IDL.DSC
idl.lsp identifies layers of entities selected in xrefs.
Donation $5
<END IDL.DSC

>ATT.DSC
att.lsp scales blocks and attributes from insertion points.
Donation $5
<END ATT.DSC

>CHCASE.DSC
chcase.lsp converts all text to uppercase in a drawing.
Donation $5
<END CHCASE.DSC

>CHSF.DSC
chsf.lsp scales all blocks within a user specified window to
a new user specified scale type 'cf'. By typing 'ctf' it will
scale all text within a user specified window.
Donation $5
<END CHSF.DSC

>NEA.DSC
nea.lsp creates a leader with the stationing (user input) angle
(calcs. from two lines selected) and northing easting (calcs).
Intended for pipe callouts in plan view.
Donation $5
<END NEA.DSC

>ROTT.DSC
rott.lsp prompts the user to window an area, it then scales every-
thing within that crossing to a user specified scale, and then 
prompts the user for a new location of the data (stretch command).
Intended for use with the nea.lsp command, to scale, rotate, and
stretch the leader and text to a new location if the dwg. size
has changed or the leader and text are in a bad location.
Donation $5
<END ROTT.DSC

>ATTE.DSC
atte.lsp replaces the annoying period (.) that is placed in the
point data of attributes for no entry text. This way it doesn't
show up if you wish to create a plot with survey elevations showing
and some elevation weren't given so softdesk put in a bunch of 
periods at point numbers where no elevations were found.
Donation $5
<END ATTE.DSC

>ATTW.DSC
attw.lsp scales attributes within blocks that are located within
a user specified window area.
Donation $5
<END ATTW.DSC

>BLE.DSC
ble.lsp erases all blocks in a drawing that have been inserted a
a certain scale (specified by user).
Donation $5
<END BLE.DSC

>CHCOL.DSC
chcol.lsp converts all entities of one color to another. (original
color can't be bylayer)
Donation $5
<END CHCOL.DSC

>CHLAY.DSC
chlay.lsp moves all entities on a selected layer to a new user
specified layer.
Donation $5
<END CHLAY.DSC

>CHLAYR.DSC
chlayr.lsp moves entities selected to the current layer.
Donation $5
<END CHLAYR.DSC

>CSTYLE.DSC
cstyle.lsp allows the user to change the style of a 
selected text entity by selecting another text entity
to match.
Donation $5
<END CSTYLE.DSC

>RETX.DSC
retx.lsp allows the user to replace old text with new text by
selection. The user is first prompted to select the text to
replace and then prompted to select the new text to replace
it with. This routine was created to update Northing and
Easting coordinates by using the softdesk label northing
easting routine and then updating the old values by selecting
the new. This eliminates the hassle of arranging new values
that have been updated to the same location and orientation as
the old values.
Donation $5
<END RETX.DSC

>VAR.DSC
Var.lsp saves a user selected group of system
variables to a file called dwgname.EVS where dwgname
is the current drawing name. These variables can then
be easily restored by selecting the restore button. If an
EVS file is not associated with the current drawing the
user will be prompted to select one. This allows the user
to match variables from one drawing to a new drawing
without prototyping. There are two main uses for this
program; 
(1) To restore the system variables which are
    often changed by Softdesk commands
    (i.e. Pickbox, Dragmode, etc..)
(2) To copy variables for one file into another
    without setting up a prototype drawing.
    (i.e. Copying Dimvars, which there are alot
    of, from a drawing where they've already
    been set to a new drawing.)
Donation $15
<END VAR.DSC

>FSTYL.DSC
fstyl.lsp was written for architectural projects to 
convert civil standard text fonts to artichoke fonts 
(Architects). This routine will search the drawing
database for all fonts that aren't either simplex, au101s01,
au102s00, or au102s01 fonts. If it finds fonts that aren't of
these types it zooms to it, highlights it, and asks the user
what style to convert it too. The Font and Style are shown at
the status area of autocad (where the coordinates and layer
are usually shown at the top of the screen.). The user must 
then type in a style that he/she wishes to use that uses one
of the fonts listed above. The program cycles through till all
fonts are checked. NOTE: if you have fonts in paperspace run
fstyl.lsp in paperspace the first time, then run it again in
model space.
Donation $5
<END FSTYL.DSC

>CHRD.DSC
chrd.lsp rounds numbers to 2 decimal places by a windowed area.
this is a quick and dirty routine with no error code so if you
mess up it will bomb out and you'll have to start over.
Donation $5
<END CHRD.DSC

>ATTPL.DSC
attpl.lsp rounds attribute numbers to 2 decimal places, it  
is intended to be used to change spot elevation attributes
to 2 places. The spot elevation block name must be named "ET".
type 'ap' to run.
Donation $5
<END ATTPL.DSC

>CHTH.DSC
chth.lsp adds thickness to text automatically for hiding with
3dfaces created with the hface routine.
Donation $5
<END CHTH.DSC

>ATTCONV.DSC
attconv.lsp changes the elevation attribute in the point block
from english units to metric using a conversion factor of 0.3048
and re-writes the number with two decimal places. You must be
using the point.dwg block however with num, elev, and desc
attributes.
Donation $5
<END ATTCONV.DSC

>RENAM.DSC
renam.lsp converts long layer names to its last 5 characters
+ a number that corresponds to its original location in the layer
list. Say you have a layer called AIA-LAYER-PRPLAN and it is the
second layer in your layer list renam.lsp renames this layer to
2PRPLAN. This routine was written so you could xref in files with
long layer names.
Donation $5
<END RENAM.DSC 

>GPEDIT.DSC
gpedit.lsp runs a global pedit command for all polylines in your
drawing. You can do a global pedit for Close polyline, Decurve,
Splinefit, Width, Fit, or Ltype.-Type GPE to run.
Donation $5
<END GPEDIT.DSC

>FTXT.DSC
ftxt.lsp finds user specified text in a drawing and zooms to that
text. This routine uses wildcard matching and allows the user
to specify the zoom window. It then tallies up the total number
of text entities in the drawing and then proceeds to zoom to each
piece of text matching the inputed parameters. type ftx to run.
Donation $5
<END FTXT.DSC

>CATTL.DSC
cattl.lsp changes attribute layers by selection. Input new layer
and select attributes to edit-type cal to begin.
Donation $5
<END CATTL.DSC

>NOEL.DSC
noel.lsp gives every entity in your drawing an elevation of zero (0)
except entities on layers dca_info, defpoints, sym*, and ----xr, and
3dpolylines.-type 'EL' to run.
Donation $5
<END NOEL.DSC

>ETXT.DSC
etxt.lsp erases all text of a certain type by selection.-select the
text 'TEXT' and every occurance of 'TEXT' is erased.
Donation $5
<END ETXT.DSC

>CHMM.DSC
chmm.lsp convert numbers in meters to mm. i.e.-> 7.90m-->7 900. Follows 
metric guidelines. Meter <space> milimeter.
Donation $5
<END CHMM.DSC

>RMM.DSC
rmm.lsp removes the 'mm' from units. i.e. 1290mm->1 290 this follows
metric conventions. Will not work for units in a sentence. i.e. 'place
90mm of A.C.'
Donation $5
<END RMM.DSC

>RETXT.DSC
retxt.lsp replaces an old text string with a new one globally-'RT' to
start.
Donation $5
<END RETXT.DSC

>CHEND.DSC
chend.lsp switches the start and endpoints of a line-type 'CE' to run.
Donation $5
<END CHEND.DSC

>ATREP.DSC
atrep.lsp replaces an old block attribute text with new user specified
text globally-type 'AR' to run.
Donation $5
<END ATREP.DSC

>BLEX.DSC
Blex.lsp explodes a block and puts its entities on the layer
that the block was inserted on. Type 'BE' to run.
Donation $5
<END BLEX.DSC

>WPEDIT.DSC
Wpedit.lsp runs a global pedit command for all polylines in that lie
within a user specified window. You can do a global pedit for 
Close polyline, Decurve, Splinefit, Width, Fit, or Ltype.
-Type WPE to run.
Donation $5
<END WPEDIT.DSC

>BRKTXT.DSC
brktxt.lsp breaks a text string at a user specified point into to text
strings. The user specifies the last 3 letters of the first string and
from this the program breaks the string into a second string. I.E. given
the string 'This is a test' if you input to break the string at 'is '
you would get the strings 'This is ' and 'a test'.
Donation $5
<END BRKTXT.DSC

>ALGTXT.DSC
algtxt.lsp aligns the dtext angle with a line by selection.
Donation $5
<END ALGTXT.DSC

>STAED.DSC
staed.lsp globally modifies the station and offset lables produced by the cogo in 
softdesk so that they read more standard. For example:
'OFFSET: 89.00 R' 
converts to 89.00' R 
and 
STA: 54+99.89 
converts to STA 54+99.89
Type 'ste' to run
Donation $5
<END STAED.DSC

>PMAKE.DSC
pmake.lsp grabs all lines (and arcs if the user specifies) in a drawing and 
converts them to polylines. It then checks all polylines and if any can be joined
 it joins them. -PMK to run
Donation $5
<END PMAKE.DSC

>PMK_L.DSC
pmk_l.lsp grabs all lines (and arcs if the user specifies) on a user selected layer, 
converts them to polylines, then joins any that can be joined.-PKL to run.
Donation $5
<END PMK_L.DSC

>SPURGE.DSC
spurge.lsp does the purge command recursivly. Just type spurge instead of purge and
you are prompted, just like the purge command, as to what you wish to purge. Spurge
then purges what you want without prompting you with a Y/N for every unreferenced
enitity, linetype, layer, block, etc. -type 'SPURGE' to run.
Donation $5
<END SPURGE.DSC

>TCLBL.DSC
tclbl.lsp requires that the blocks 'SL','SR','CL', & 'CR' be defined in your current
drawing. These block must have a top of curb attribute tag and a bottom of curb attr.
tag. This routine prompts the user for the top of curb elevation and then insertion
point and angle. It then calcs. the bottom of curb (BC) and inserts one of the blocks
listed above with the TC and BC attributes filled in. Type 'SL', 'SR', 'CL', or 'CR' 
to run depending on which block you wish to insert. 'SL' would be a slanted left tag,
'SR' would be Slanted right, 'CL' & 'CR' would have the text facing opposite 
(upside down) from 'SR' and 'SL'.
Donation $5
<END TCLBL.DSC 

>ROTXT.DSC
rotxt.lsp rotates text about mid point.-type 'RTX'
Donation $5
<END ROTXT.DSC

>AT_ADD.DSC
at_add.lsp adds a user specified factor to the point attribute of the point.dwg block.
It doesn't act globally but through a user specified windowed area. This was written
so I could insert a block from another file and project that has points in it and run
the softdesk check points routine to add the points to the current drawings project
without having a duplicate points error.
Donation $5
<END AT_ADD.DSC

>ATORDER.DSC
atorder.lsp reorders attribute tags. It asks a user to specify a block with the 
correct attribute tag order. It then proceeds to reorder all the attribute tags
of all the blocks. (does a maximum of 4 attributes per block.)
Donation $5
<END ATORDER.DSC

>CHSLANT.DSC
chslant.lsp changes text slant globally-CHSL to run.
Donation $5
<END CHSLANT.DSC

>CNT.DSC
cnt.lsp inserts sequentail numbered text into a drawing. Input starting number, factor
to count up or down by, whether to count up or down, and then just start picking points
to insert the text.-Type 'KCN'.
Donation $5
<END CNT.DSC

>CSPACE.DSC
Cspace.lsp Changes the space of a windowed selection of entities. If they're in
model space they are moved to paper space or visa versa.
Donation $5
<END CSPACE.DSC

>CLEAN.DSC
Clean.lsp is useful if you ever try to zoom extents and find Autocad zooms out to 
la la land but you cant find any entities out there. You probably have either null
text entities (text entities with no text thus not visible) or entities consisting 
of only spaces (again not visible). This routine finds all of these entities and 
gets rid of them for you - Type ''CLEAN' to run.
Donation $5
<END CLEAN.DSC

>SRVY.DSC
Srvy.lsp is a combination of the dw.lsp, xnut.lsp, xfl.lsp, and sta.lsp routines in a
dialogue box interface. Using this program you can insert driveways, watermeters 
elevations, flowlines, and stationing automatically into your drawing. This program
operates by reading the *.pts file associated with the drawing. This program also 
reads the user inputed scale, rotation, basepoint, and north rotation. See the
programs online help for more info.
Donation $10
<END SRVY.DSC

>XRUT.DSC
Xrut.lsp is a set of layer manipulation routines. The following routines are
available:
              1)IX-Isolates layers by selection.
              2)RI-Identify layers by selection.
              3)XF-Freeze layers by selection.

These routines allow you to not only perform these functions on entity layers 
in your current drawing but also xref'd layers.
